title: The Blockade
subtitle: The Northern Fleet contests the sea lanes, and our shipping is caught in between...
new-page: true
view-if: year = 1932 and month >= 6 and not blockade_seen
max-visits: 1
priority: 2
tags: event
on-arrival: {!
Q.blockade_seen = 1;

// who, if anyone, is in a position to answer this
Q.blockade_responsive = (Q.chancellor_party === "Unionist" || Q.chancellor_party === "LVP" || Q.chancellor_party === "Gold") ? 1 : 0;
var _strongMilitary = (Q.military_readiness || 0) >= 200;

// the confrontation flatters the Restorationists whoever is in office
Q.commons_royalist = (Q.commons_royalist || 0) + 2;
Q.burgher_royalist = (Q.burgher_royalist || 0) + 2;
Q.faithful_royalist = (Q.faithful_royalist || 0) + 2;
Q.loyalist_strength = (Q.loyalist_strength || 0) + 25;

if (Q.marcher_in_government) {
    // ours to answer, and ours to pay for
    Q.budget -= 1;
    Q.dufour_angry = (Q.dufour_angry || 0) + 5;
} else if (Q.blockade_responsive) {
    if (_strongMilitary) {
        Q.blockade_state_acted = 1;
    } else {
        Q.budget -= 1;
        Q.blockade_state_complained = 1;
    }
} else {
    Q.budget -= 1;
}
!}

= The Blockade

The <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Empire-in-Exile</span>'s Northern Fleet is no longer the coastal force it was two years ago. It has doubled in size, it has spent the spring on exercises, and it has begun stopping merchant ships in the straits, politely, with full paperwork, and on its own authority.

<span style="color: #D3C24D;">**Ethia**</span> will not concede the sea lanes and has sent its own squadrons north. Neither fleet has fired. Both are now inspecting the same waters, and Commonwealth ships carrying Commonwealth grain are being boarded by both in turn, sometimes on the same voyage.

[? if marcher_in_government: The losses fall on the government. Cargoes are impounded, insurance rates on the northern run have tripled, and the Treasury is short by the end of the week. President DuFour wants to know what the government intends to do. ?][? if not marcher_in_government and blockade_state_acted: [+ chancellor +] does not hesitate. The Galleon Fleet is ordered north to escort our shipping through, and it is strong enough that both parties to the quarrel decide to be elsewhere. The convoys get through, the Treasury holds, and the credit is entirely his. ?][? if not marcher_in_government and blockade_state_complained: [+ chancellor +] does what a man without a fleet worth the name can do. He lodges a complaint, and lodges it again, and the impounded cargoes stay impounded. The Treasury is short by the end of the week. ?][? if not marcher_in_government and not blockade_responsive: [+ chancellor +] treats the affair as beneath comment. No protest is lodged, no escort is ordered, and the shipping interest is told to make its own arrangements. The country absorbs the losses. ?]

Whoever is at fault, the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> are winning the argument in every tavern on the coast. Their line is simple: an empire that can seize our grain is an empire we should not have left. Their support rises and the Loyalists gain recruits.

- @runners
- @complaints
- @land_trade
- @negotiate
- @outrageous
- @push_chancellor
- @empire_closer

@runners
title: Send for blockade runners.
subtitle: Escort the convoys with the Galleon Fleet.
view-if: marcher_in_government
choose-if: military_readiness >= 200
unavailable-subtitle: Our forces are nowhere near strong enough to contest the straits.
on-arrival: budget += 1; dufour_angry -= 5; commons_royalist -= 2; burgher_royalist -= 2; faithful_royalist -= 2; loyalist_strength -= 25

We order the Galleon Fleet north with instructions to escort our shipping through and to treat any interference as hostile. Neither the Imperials nor the Ethians care to test whether we mean it, and the boardings stop within the fortnight.

The cargoes come in, the insurance rates fall, and the Treasury is whole. The argument in the coastal taverns changes as well. It is hard to sell the claim that the Commonwealth cannot protect its own ships while its own ships are visibly being protected. Royalist support falls and DuFour is satisfied.

@complaints
title: Lodge formal complaints.
subtitle: Requires the chancellery or the Foreign Ministry. Bring our allies into the dispute.
view-if: marcher_in_government
choose-if: foreign_minister_party == "Marcher" or chancellor_party == "Marcher"
unavailable-subtitle: We must hold the chancellery or the Foreign Ministry to act on the Commonwealth's behalf.
on-arrival: west_relation += 1; east_relation += 1; nationalism -= 1

We take the case to every capital that will hear it: notes to <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**Ethia**</span>, notes to the <span style="color: #708090;">**Allied States**</span>, a formal protest at the Assembly of Realms, and a good deal of quiet work reminding both that a fleet which stops our grain this year will stop theirs next year.

This recovers nothing that was taken. It does establish the Commonwealth, for the first time since the Fall, as a party to the northern question rather than an object of it. Relations with both the West and the North improve.

@land_trade
title: Pivot towards land-based trade.
subtitle: Requires an economic ministry and a friend abroad. Route around the sea entirely.
view-if: marcher_in_government
choose-if: (chancellor_party == "Marcher" or finance_minister_party == "Marcher" or foreign_minister_party == "Marcher") and (west_relation >= 2 or east_relation >= 2)
unavailable-subtitle: [? if chancellor_party != "Marcher" and finance_minister_party != "Marcher" and foreign_minister_party != "Marcher": We hold none of the ministries that could arrange it. ?][? if west_relation < 2 and east_relation < 2: No neighbour trusts us enough to open its roads to our freight. ?]
on-arrival: budget += 1; economic_growth += 0.1

If the straits are closed, we will not use the straits. Freight is redirected overland through friendly territory. It is slower and dearer per ton, and entirely beyond the reach of anyone's navy. The arrangement takes three weeks to negotiate and pays for itself before the quarter is out. The Treasury recovers and growth improves slightly.

@negotiate
title: Negotiate directly with the Empire-in-Exile.
subtitle: Requires the chancellery or the Foreign Ministry. Deal directly with the fleet that is stopping us.
view-if: marcher_in_government
choose-if: (foreign_minister_party == "Marcher" or chancellor_party == "Marcher") and empire_posture >= 40
unavailable-subtitle: [? if foreign_minister_party != "Marcher" and chancellor_party != "Marcher": We must hold the chancellery or the Foreign Ministry to open talks. ?][? if empire_posture < 40: The Imperial court is in no humour to receive us. ?]
on-arrival: budget += 1; empire_posture += 8 if empire_posture <= 92; gold_relation += 5; lvp_relation += 5; dufour_angry -= 5; left_dissent += 10; collectivist_relation -= 8; collectivist_coalition_dissent += 1; coalition_dissent += 1 if in_popular_front or in_left_front

We go to them directly, and they are entirely reasonable about it. A schedule of exemptions for Commonwealth ships is signed within the month, on the understanding that our ships are not carrying Ethian cargo under another flag. The impounded grain is released and the Treasury is whole.

The <span style="color: #C0A054;">**Golds**</span> are delighted. This is exactly the unsentimental commercial diplomacy they have been asking for, and relations with them improve. Even the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span> manage a word of praise for a government that finally spoke to Axton as an equal, though it does nothing to slow their recruiting. The Empire's posture warms considerably and DuFour is pleased.

Our own Left is furious. We have signed a maritime agreement with the power that took Ivion, on its terms. Left dissent rises sharply, and the <span style="color: #700000;">**Collectivists**</span> are already printing the word accommodation in the largest type they own. Relations with them fall.

@outrageous
title: Condemn the interference, and do nothing else.
subtitle: Issue a statement. Take no action.
view-if: marcher_in_government

The government issues a statement condemning the interference with neutral shipping in the strongest available terms, and there the matter rests. The boardings continue.

@push_chancellor
title: Push the Chancellor towards a stronger line.
subtitle: Requires good relations with his party. He has no fleet, but he has allies.
view-if: not marcher_in_government and blockade_responsive
choose-if: (chancellor_party == "Unionist" and unionist_relation >= 55) or ((chancellor_party == "LVP" or chancellor_party == "Gold") and lvp_relation >= 55)
unavailable-subtitle: [+ chancellor +] does not owe us the hearing, and will not take one.
on-arrival: west_relation += 1; east_relation += 1; nationalism -= 1

We are not in the government, but we are on good terms with the men who are. Our people put the case to [+ chancellor +] privately: a complaint delivered alone is noise, and a complaint delivered alongside <span style="color: #D3C24D;">**Ethia**</span> and the <span style="color: #708090;">**Allied States**</span> is a position.

He takes the advice and takes the credit, which is the usual arrangement. The Commonwealth ends the month with allies it did not have at the start of it. Relations with the West and the North improve.

@empire_closer
title: Speak from opposition. There is nothing more we can do.
subtitle: The government is not ours, and neither is the fleet.
view-if: not marcher_in_government

We say what an opposition can say, at length and to no effect. The fleet stays where it is. The government stays what it is. The grain sits in an Imperial harbour under a courteous receipt, and every week it stays there the argument moves further from us.
